I suggest a slight modification, I think 13 games might be a little
much, particuarly for anyone shooting for a high percentage. Consider
that last year the highest number of ascensions was 22 and that most
people probably did not do anywhere near this many. 

I suggest, instead, a minimum of 8 games with some restriction in
class choice for anyone not completing the full 13:

Barbarian, Caveman, Valkyrie, and Samurai are generally considered the
easiest classes to get off the ground, which is basically all it takes
for a decent player to ascend failing a YASD. Only count them for the
% if a player has completed at least 2 games (of other classes) for
each 1 of these he plays.

That is to say, if a player could have as his 8 games:

Healer, Wizard, [Samurai], Tourist, Rogue, [Barbarian], Ranger, Monk

You could also round up if you think this is too harsh:

[Samurai], Healer, Wizard, [Barbarian], Tourist, Rogue, [Valkyrie],
Ranger

Another way of putting it is: Bar/Cav/Val/Sam can take up no more than
1/3 of the scoresheet

And I could see an argument, honestly, for requiring this percentage
to be maintained at any degree, although it is considerably less
essential past 8.


I just personally feel that if you put the number at 13, a lot of
people won't be able to participate because, I mean, I know I don't
have any intenteion of playing 13 full games. 8 I could see -- maybe,
it's still going to be pushing the envelope but I can't really see a
lower number being fair, 12.5% increments is, itself, getting on the
heavy side.


Another possible, and more simply, restriction would be to require
players to play until they lost at least 1 game, or if they do not do
so by the end of the tournament, add a 1 game forfeiture to their
score.

This has the added bonus of making it improbable that there would be
more than 1 winner.

One could argue that this favors someone who plays a whole lot of
games, but you have to remember, they have to win those games in order
to only take a 1 game hit, and if someone wins 50 games in a row and
only loses 2% and another person wins only 15 games and takes a 6.25%
hit I think it's fair to say that the person who won 50 games did a
better job.

If anyone has a serious problem with this solution another way of
doing it would be to not apply the hit if a player has played more
than 13 games, and only apply it to players who played fewer. 

This would allow for what you said, requiring 13 games to score well,
while allowing players who played fewer games to participate on a less
spectuacular level.

I dunno, lots of ins and outs, just my various 2 cents.

